# Security Review Skill

This skill enforces practical security checks for this Laravel + Inertia + Vue application.

## Version Snapshot (Verified Mar 19, 2026)

- PHP 8.4.17
- Laravel 12.55.1
- inertiajs/inertia-laravel 2.0.x
- @inertiajs/vue3 2.3.x
- Vue 3.5.x (Options API is the default for new work)
- Tailwind CSS 4.2.x
- Pest 3.8.x

## When to Activate

- Implementing authentication or authorization logic.
- Handling user input, uploads, webhooks, or third-party callbacks.
- Building or reviewing API endpoints and mutation flows.
- Working with secrets, credentials, billing, or sensitive data.
- Performing code review with security as a primary concern.

## Documentation-First Workflow (Required)

1. Use Laravel Boost `search-docs` first for Laravel/Inertia/Pest/Tailwind security behavior.
2. Use Context7 as supplemental documentation (for Vue and non-Laravel libraries).
3. If docs and module conventions conflict, follow touched-module conventions and document the trade-off.

## Project Reality Overrides (Highest Priority)

- Laravel runtime wiring is centered in `bootstrap/app.php` and `bootstrap/providers.php`.
- Backend frequently uses `app/Services/**` classes with `*Class` naming and controller `option` branching.
- Authorization boundaries are often route middleware + role checks; policy usage is partial.
- Frontend is JS-first Vue Options API with Inertia + axios coexistence.
- Security boundaries are always enforced server-side, even if frontend hides UI actions.

## Security Review Order

1. Access control (authn/authz/ownership/tenant boundaries).
2. Input handling (validation/mass assignment/query safety/uploads).
3. Output handling (XSS/template safety/sensitive payload minimization).
4. Request protection (CSRF/rate limiting/replay abuse).
5. Operational hygiene (logs/errors/secrets/dependency risk).

## Critical Security Checks

### 1) Secrets and Configuration

- Never hardcode API keys, passwords, or tokens.
- Use `config(...)` in app code, and keep `env(...)` usage only in config files.
- Never expose secrets via Inertia props, JS bundles, or logs.

```php
// BAD
$apiKey = 'sk-live-123';

// GOOD
$apiKey = config('services.openai.key');
```

### 2) Authentication, Authorization, and IDOR

- Require authentication for protected routes/actions.
- Enforce ownership and tenant boundaries on every read/write.
- Keep frontend checks as UX-only; server-side checks are mandatory.

```php
public function show(Request $request, Project $project): \Inertia\Response
{
    abort_unless($request->user(), 401);

    if ($project->organization_id !== $request->user()->organization_id) {
        abort(404);
    }

    $this->authorize('view', $project);

    return \Inertia\Inertia::render('projects/Show', [
        'project' => $project->only(['id', 'name', 'status']),
    ]);
}
```

### 3) Validation and Mass Assignment

- Use Form Requests for complex mutation flows.
- Use `$request->validated()` or `$request->safe()`; never `$request->all()` for writes.
- Keep model fill/guard strategy explicit to prevent privilege escalation.

```php
public function store(StoreUserRequest $request): \Illuminate\Http\RedirectResponse
{
    User::query()->create($request->validated());

    return to_route('users.index');
}
```

### 4) SQL Injection and Query Safety

- Prefer Eloquent/query builder parameterization.
- Avoid concatenating user input into SQL fragments.
- Allowlist dynamic `orderBy` columns and direction.

```php
// BAD
$users = DB::select("SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '{$email}'");

// GOOD
$users = User::query()->where('email', $email)->get();
```

### 5) XSS and Vue Rendering Safety

- Use `{{ value }}` interpolation by default (escaped output).
- Avoid `v-html`; if unavoidable, sanitize trusted HTML before rendering.
- Treat URL/style/script bindings from user input as untrusted.
- Never mount Vue to server-rendered user HTML fragments.

```vue
<template>
    <p>{{ userProvidedText }}</p>
    <!-- Only render sanitized, trusted HTML -->
    <div v-if="trustedHtml" v-html="trustedHtml"></div>
</template>
```

### 6) CSRF and Session Security

- Keep state-changing routes behind CSRF protection.
- Ensure no GET endpoint performs state changes.
- Use secure session cookie settings (`secure`, `http_only`, `same_site`).
- For axios/Inertia mutations, ensure CSRF token flow remains intact.

### 7) File Upload Security

- Validate file type and size server-side using Laravel validation rules.
- Prefer `File` rule builder for explicitness.
- Store with generated names (do not trust user-provided file names).
- Prefer private storage unless public access is explicitly required.

```php
use Illuminate\Validation\Rules\File;

$validated = $request->validate([
    'avatar' => [
        'required',
        File::image()
            ->types(['jpg', 'jpeg', 'png', 'webp'])
            ->max(5 * 1024),
    ],
]);

$path = $request->file('avatar')->store('avatars', 'private');
```

### 8) Open Redirect, Path Traversal, SSRF

- Redirects: allow relative paths only.
- File access: map identifiers to approved files; do not accept raw paths.
- Outbound fetches: allowlist hostnames and block private/internal ranges.

```php
$redirect = (string) $request->input('redirect', '/dashboard');
abort_unless(str_starts_with($redirect, '/') && ! str_starts_with($redirect, '//'), 400);

return redirect()->to($redirect);
```

### 9) Rate Limiting and Abuse Controls

- Apply throttles to login, password reset, OTP, webhook, and expensive endpoints.
- Use stricter limits for unauthenticated and high-cost operations.
- Prefer deterministic keys (`user id` or `ip`) for limiter buckets.

### 10) Sensitive Data Exposure

- Do not log passwords, tokens, OTPs, full card data, or secrets.
- Avoid leaking stack traces and internal details to end users.
- Minimize payloads returned to Inertia pages and JSON responses.

```php
return Inertia::render('profile/Show', [
    'user' => $request->user()?->only(['id', 'name', 'email']),
]);
```

### 11) Dependency and Supply-Chain Security

- Audit backend and frontend dependencies regularly.
- Commit lock files and use reproducible installs in CI.
- Prefer patched versions when vulnerabilities are disclosed.

## Security Testing Requirements (Pest)

- Add or update tests for:
    - authentication and authorization boundaries,
    - validation failures (including file rules),
    - ownership/tenant isolation,
    - rate limiting behavior,
    - sensitive fields excluded from responses.
- Use specific response assertions (`assertUnauthorized`, `assertForbidden`, `assertUnprocessable`, etc.).
- For Inertia endpoints, assert component + prop safety with `assertInertia`.

```php
it('blocks non-admin from deleting users', function () {
    $user = User::factory()->create();
    $target = User::factory()->create();

    actingAs($user)
        ->delete(route('users.destroy', $target))
        ->assertForbidden();
});
```

## Quick Security Commands

```bash
# Focused verification
php artisan test --compact --filter=Security

# Formatting
./vendor/bin/pint --dirty --format agent

# Dependency audits
composer audit
npm audit

# Fast pattern checks
rg "->all\(|request\(\)->all\(" app
rg "DB::raw|selectRaw|whereRaw" app
rg "v-html" resources/js
rg "redirect\\(.*request\\(" app
```

## Pre-Merge Security Checklist

- [ ] No hardcoded secrets, keys, or credentials.
- [ ] Access control enforced server-side for every sensitive action.
- [ ] Input validation and allowlists in place for all mutation paths.
- [ ] No mass assignment risks from unvalidated data.
- [ ] XSS risks addressed (`v-html` avoided or sanitized).
- [ ] CSRF/session protections preserved for state-changing requests.
- [ ] Upload validations include type and size, plus safe storage strategy.
- [ ] Redirect/path/SSRF-sensitive flows are constrained.
- [ ] Logs and errors do not expose sensitive internals.
- [ ] Security-related tests exist and pass.

